/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin-allow-popups
  Origin-Agent-Cluster: ?1
  Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://datafa.st https://cdn.mxpnl.com https://www.googletagmanager.com https://static.cloudflareinsights.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: blob: https://cdn.picxstudio.com https://lh3.googleusercontent.com; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' wss: https://api.picxstudio.com https://api-js.mixpanel.com https://www.google-analytics.com https://datafa.st https://cloudflareinsights.com; worker-src 'self' blob:; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why these two headers look the way they do
#
# Origin-Agent-Cluster — required by WebMCP, not a nice-to-have. The spec's
# registerTool() steps reject with a SecurityError unless the document's agent
# cluster `is origin-keyed` (the only exception being file: URLs), and Chrome's
# WebMCP docs state the API is available only in origin-isolated documents.
# Chrome already defaults to origin-keyed clusters, so this header does not
# change today's behaviour — it PINS it, so a future default change or a stray
# `Origin-Agent-Cluster: ?0` cannot silently unregister every tool on the site.
# The trade-off is giving up document.domain mutation, which this app never uses.
#
# Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy — the 2026-08-28 audit rated "No COOP header found"
# High. `same-origin-allow-popups` rather than plain `same-origin` on purpose:
# `same-origin` severs window.opener for popups, and while Better Auth's Google
# sign-in currently uses a full redirect (auth-client.ts sets
# window.location.href), a popup-based flow is a plausible future change and this
# value satisfies the audit either way.
#
# Content-Security-Policy — REPORT-ONLY, deliberately. The audit rated "No CSP
# found in enforcement mode" High, but shipping an enforcing CSP straight to
# production on a site with inline scripts breaks it instantly. Report-Only
# collects violations while changing nothing.
#
#   Origins allowed above, and what each is for:
#     datafa.st                   DataFast pageview script
#     cdn.mxpnl.com               Mixpanel library + the lazily-injected Session
#                                 Replay recorder bundle (added as a <script>, so
#                                 script-src covers it — no connect-src entry needed)
#     api-js.mixpanel.com         Mixpanel event ingest AND replay data upload
#                                 (Session Replay reuses api_host; there is no
#                                 separate replay endpoint)
#     www.googletagmanager.com    GA4 (gtag.js)
#     www.google-analytics.com    GA4 collect endpoint
#     static.cloudflareinsights.com / cloudflareinsights.com   CF Web Analytics
#     cdn.picxstudio.com          all generated + skill-card imagery
#     lh3.googleusercontent.com   Google account avatars after OAuth sign-in
#     api.picxstudio.com          generation + managed asset uploads
#     wss:                        @tldraw/sync WebSocket to the RoadmapRoom /
#                                 BoardRoom Durable Objects
#
# BEFORE THIS CAN BE PROMOTED TO ENFORCEMENT, two things must change:
#
#   1. `script-src 'unsafe-inline'` has to go. As of the Session Replay migration
#      there is exactly ONE inline script left in AppLayout.astro — the pre-paint
#      theme setter, which must stay synchronous and inline to avoid a light-mode
#      flash — plus the GA4 gtag bootstrap. (The Mixpanel loader stub that used to
#      be the other blocker is gone: Mixpanel is now a bundled npm module, see
#      src/scripts/app/analytics-boot.ts.) Replace the remaining two with
#      per-request nonces or SHA-256 hashes — not by deleting the scripts.
#   2. `style-src 'unsafe-inline'` is required by Astro's scoped <style> blocks
#      and by build.inlineStylesheets: 'always' in astro.config.mjs. Astro has no
#      nonce story for these yet, so this one may have to stay; that is an
#      accepted, documented residual risk rather than an oversight.
#
# There is no report-uri/report-to endpoint, so violations currently surface only
# in the browser console. Wiring a collector is a prerequisite for promotion —
# otherwise "the reports are clean" is unfalsifiable. Owner: Nema (policy) / Kai
# (rollout).
#
# NOT ADDED — HSTS `preload`. The audit rated its absence Medium. Adding it is
# effectively irreversible: submission to the browser preload list pins HTTPS for
# the max-age window (one year here) across doodleai.art AND every subdomain via
# includeSubDomains, so any future subdomain that cannot serve valid TLS becomes
# unreachable with no quick rollback. That needs an explicit decision, not an
# agent's.
#
# Trusted Types (`require-trusted-types-for 'script'`) is also still missing and
# was rated High. It is a larger change than a header line — every DOM sink in
# src/scripts/app/ that assigns innerHTML would need a policy — so it is tracked
# separately rather than half-added here.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# No /fonts/* rule: the site uses the system font stack and ships no font files.
# Astro's own hashed output under /_astro/* is already immutable by filename.
/_astro/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
